cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "edaphos: Digital Soil Mapping with Causal, Physics-Informed and Generative Methods"
abstract: >-
  edaphos is a research-oriented R package for digital soil mapping
  beyond regression-tree models, organised around ten methodological
  pillars: (1) causal inference -- backdoor adjustment on pedogenetic
  directed acyclic graphs, instrumental variables with first-stage and
  Sargan diagnostics, Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity analysis, and
  LLM-assisted causal-claim extraction; (2) physics-informed depth
  modelling with a closed-form pedogenetic ODE and Neural ODEs;
  (3) 4D pedometry with stacked ConvLSTM forecasting and Ensemble
  Kalman Filter assimilation; (4) foundation models pre-trained by
  SimCLR / MoCo-v2 contrastive learning on raster patches; (5) active
  learning with physics-informed rejection gates; (6) quantum machine
  learning with a ZZFeatureMap kernel simulator; (7) Bayesian
  hierarchical spatial models; (8) neural operators (DeepONet, Fourier
  Neural Operator); (9) denoising diffusion models for soil-map
  patches; and (10) graph attention networks. A common
  edaphos_posterior class lets seven of the ten pillars be scored with
  the same calibration diagnostics (CRPS, PICP, MPIW). The
  deep-learning pillars use torch when libtorch is available and a
  pure-R reference path otherwise. Real-data benchmarks on WoSIS
  profiles from the Brazilian Cerrado are reproducible from the
  scripts in data-raw/; the package ships aggregate results, not
  third-party soil measurements. The reported results include
  negative ones: foundation-embedding instruments are strong but fail
  the Sargan over-identification test, and on the real-data benchmark
  several frontier pillars do not outperform a DAG-informed linear
  model.
